WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.280
One kind of cell, the hair cell, tells you that a violin is playing an A. A hair

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.120
cell turns a tiny movement into a graded voltage.

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.000
But how does a coiled tube turn pitch into something a cell can read?

4
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.960
Uncoil the cochlea, and the answer runs along its length.

5
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.440
Frequency is turned into position.

6
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:27.073
Cochlear hair cells sit on the basilar membrane, which runs the length of the coil.

7
00:00:27.773 --> 00:00:31.793
Near the base, where sound enters, the membrane is narrow and stiff.

8
00:00:32.593 --> 00:00:34.953
Toward the apex it is wider and more compliant.

9
00:00:35.613 --> 00:00:38.793
So the membrane has a stiffness gradient from base to apex.

10
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.947
Sound entering at the base sets up a travelling wave.

11
00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:47.607
It moves from base to apex, and grows until it reaches the place whose stiffness

12
00:00:47.607 --> 00:00:48.627
suits its frequency.

13
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:51.187
There it peaks, and then it dies away.

14
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:53.567
High frequencies peak near the base.

15
00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.027
Low frequencies peak near the apex.

16
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.170
Greenwood's function gives the place of each frequency in the human cochlea.

17
00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.910
a cochlea 35 mm long.

18
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:10.250
A tone of 1,000 Hz peaks 14.0 mm from the apex, which is 21.0 mm from the base.

19
00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:16.350
A tone of 4,000 Hz peaks 11.7 mm from the base, nearer than the lower tone.

20
00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:21.570
These two tones, two octaves apart, sit 9.3 mm apart.

21
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:27.203
Von Békésy measured these waves, many of them in human cadaver cochleas.

22
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:30.423
His peaks were far broader than the tuning of a living ear.

23
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:32.363
The difference is an amplifier.

24
00:01:33.323 --> 00:01:37.563
Outer hair cells change length when their voltage changes, and on each cycle of a

25
00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:39.643
sound they push on the membrane in step.

26
00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:44.503
This adds energy at the peak, which sharpens tuning and raises sensitivity.

27
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:49.117
This map of frequency onto position is called tonotopy.

28
00:01:49.697 --> 00:01:54.397
The auditory nerve ends in the cochlear nucleus, then come the superior olivary

29
00:01:54.397 --> 00:01:56.717
complex and the inferior colliculus.

30
00:01:57.377 --> 00:02:01.117
The medial geniculate nucleus then relays to auditory cortex.

31
00:02:01.937 --> 00:02:06.657
Keep this rule: frequency becomes position, kept as a coarse map through the main

32
00:02:06.657 --> 00:02:07.137
relays.
